## Interest-Influence Analysis

The RCHN Chief Medical Officer has high interest and high influence because the new intake and scheduling system can affect clinical workflows, patient information, and how efficiently the six clinics operate. The CMO can also provide clinical direction and raise concerns about changes that could affect patient care. The appropriate strategy is to manage this stakeholder closely through regular involvement and communication.

The RCHN Board of Directors has high influence but lower day-to-day interest. The Board can provide organizational support and influence major decisions about the project, but they are not directly using the intake and scheduling system. The appropriate strategy is to keep them satisfied with executive-level updates about project progress, major concerns, and important decisions.

## Engagement Strategy Reflection

### Highest Priority Stakeholder

The RCHN Chief Medical Officer requires the most deliberate engagement because the new intake and scheduling system will affect clinical workflows and patient care across the six clinics. If the CMO's concerns are overlooked, the project could create workflow problems, resistance to the new system, or issues that affect patient services. Keeping the CMO involved can help the project team identify clinical concerns early and make better decisions about the system.

### Risk of Omission

One stakeholder group I considered but did not include is patients. Patients are directly affected by the intake and scheduling process, but I focused this register on stakeholders who have a more direct role in project decisions, implementation, or organizational support. I would add patients as a stakeholder group if RCHN provides information showing that patient feedback is required for the system design. Surveys, patient interviews, or evidence of recurring patient complaints about scheduling would also be reasons to add them to the register.
